DTLS read path, handshake retransmission, ICE candidate de-duplication,
per-pair ICE diagnostics and REMB for libpeer (extern/libpeer).

extern/libpeer is a git SUBMODULE, so its sources must not be edited in
place: the next `git submodule update` would throw the changes away.  This
patch is applied to the checked out tree by
tools/apply-libpeer-switch-patches.sh, right before libpeer is built (it is
the eighth patch of that series, after 0001-switch-webrtc-fixes,
0002-no-stdout-log, 0003-h265-depacketizer, 0004-dtls-diagnostics,
0005-ice-roles-prflx-and-timing, 0006-dtls-fingerprint-and-verify and
0007-ice-timeout-and-verify-none).

Why
---
0) The DTLS READ PATH must not block and must never return 0.

   The read path is the place where a DTLS handshake dies silently.
   mbedTLS reads a 0 from the bio as "the peer closed the connection"
   (MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_CONN_EOF == -0x7280) and a blocking read in the bio
   stalls the whole peer connection loop -- and with it the ICE
   retransmission timer and the media pump.

   What we found in OUR tree (not assumed, checked):

   a) The bio that is really used is `peer_connection_dtls_srtp_recv()`
      (src/peer_connection.c), installed at peer_connection.c:1364 right
      after dtls_srtp_init().  It already returns
      MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_WANT_READ when there is no data and never returns 0,
      so the "stuck on negotiating connecting / -0x7280" symptom of the
      other stack does NOT happen on our peer connection path.

   b) The DEFAULT bio, dtls_srtp_udp_recv() (src/dtls_srtp.c), DID block:
      it spun in `while (recvfrom() <= 0) ports_sleep_ms(1)` until a
      datagram arrived, i.e. forever.  It never returned 0, but it blocked
      the whole thread.  It also overwrote `udp_socket->bind_addr` (OUR
      local address) with the source address of the datagram.  It is now a
      non blocking read that answers MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_WANT_READ.

   c) ★ The REAL defect in our tree is the same family as the other
      stack's "the blocking read kills the retransmission timer", but the
      cause is different: dtls_srtp_do_handshake() re-registered the
      mbedTLS timer callback on EVERY handshake step.  peer_connection_loop()
      runs once per rendered frame, and mbedtls_ssl_set_timer_cb() ends
      with mbedtls_ssl_set_timer(ssl, 0) -- it CANCELS the running timer.
      The DTLS retransmission timer was therefore cancelled ~30 times per
      second and the handshake flight was NEVER retransmitted: one lost
      ClientHello killed the handshake until peer_connection.c gave the
      endpoint up after 3.5 s and restarted ICE.

      The callback is now registered ONCE, in dtls_srtp_init().

   d) The CRITERIA of this part (AGENTS.md rule 8: the criterion has to
      change when the fault happens):
        dtls_bio_eof_returns  -- how many reads returned 0.  MUST STAY 0.
                                It goes red the moment somebody puts a
                                `return 0` back into the read path.
        dtls_bio_want_reads   -- the reads that found no data (normal, it
                                grows during a healthy handshake).
        dtls_bio_read_calls   -- every read.
        dtls_bio_max_call_us  -- the worst single read.  A blocking read
                                shows up here immediately.
      All four are counted in ONE place (dtls_srtp_bio_recv(), which
      wraps whichever callback is installed) and are reported through
      getStats().

   e) The ICE timers are NOT affected by the read path: the ICE
      retransmission schedule of 0037/0039 is a WALL CLOCK deadline
      (ports_get_epoch_time() - last_conncheck_sent_ms), not a loop
      count, so a slow read cannot shift it.  The only thing a blocking
      read could do is DELAY the moment the deadline is noticed -- which
      is why the read path must not block in the first place.

1) The DTLS handshake needs a retransmission schedule and a bounded MTU.

   mbedtls_ssl_conf_handshake_timeout() was never called: the defaults
   (1000/60000) were in force, and -- because of the timer cancellation
   above -- even those never fired.  It is now called with 1000/4000:
   the flight is repeated after 1 s, 3 s and 7 s and then mbedTLS gives
   up with MBEDTLS_ERR_SSL_TIMEOUT.  4 s is the value the other stack
   uses; it is short enough to fit into our 14 s ICE budget and our 9 s
   DTLS endpoint budget.

   peer_connection.c gave a DTLS endpoint up after 3500 ms -- BEFORE the
   second retransmission (3 s) could arrive.  It is now 9000 ms, i.e. the
   endpoint timeout is the backstop and mbedTLS's own schedule is the
   primary deadline.

   mbedtls_ssl_set_mtu(&ssl, 1200) caps the records we send.  The peer
   overwrites ssl->mtu with the value from ITS ClientHello, but we are
   the DTLS client, so that cannot happen here; the value is re-asserted
   before every handshake step anyway.

2) The ICE candidate pairs are de-duplicated per remote ENDPOINT and the
   unreachable remote addresses are checked LAST.

   agent_update_candidate_pairs() built the full local x remote product.
   With M local candidates and N remote candidates that is M*N pairs, and
   libpeer checks ONE pair at a time with a 3 s budget each -- so a cloud
   that publishes 3 addresses and a host with 3 local candidates spends
   9 x 3 s = 27 s on a round whose budget is 14 s, and only a third of the
   addresses ever get checked.

   Now there is exactly ONE pair per remote endpoint (ip:port), built with
   the local candidate of the highest priority.  The peer reflexive path
   (agent_pair_remote_candidate()) goes through the same de-duplication,
   so a peer reflexive candidate can no longer duplicate the pair of a
   remote address that is already in the table.

   The pairs are sorted by (reachability, priority): a remote address
   that a peer outside of our LAN can not be reached at (RFC 1918,
   loopback, link local, "any") is checked LAST.  Such a candidate is NOT
   deleted -- it is only demoted, because a cloud that publishes a LAN
   candidate IS reachable when both ends are on the same LAN.

   ★ HONEST: the miHoYo cloud currently publishes three PUBLIC addresses
     (119.6.222.11 / 182.138.254.11 / 112.45.114.203), so for the data we
     have today the reachability rank changes NOTHING -- every candidate
     gets rank 0.  It is a defensive mechanism, not the fix for the
     problem we are hitting right now.

3) The ICE diagnostics that answer "did the cloud answer THIS address?".

   F1  IceCandidatePair.responses_received -- the binding responses that
       this pair received.  The engine could only prove that it handed a
       candidate to the native layer, never that the cloud answered it.
       With one pair per remote endpoint this number IS the "how often did
       the cloud answer this IP" number.
   F2  agent.rejected_binding_responses -- the responses that arrived and
       were DROPPED.  The counter existed but did NOT cover the failed
       integrity check (the old code dropped those silently), so "the
       cloud answered and we threw the answer away" and "the cloud never
       answered" looked identical.  It does not any more.
   F3  agent.first_conncheck_ms -- the wall clock of the FIRST
       connectivity check that really went out.  The delay between "the
       answer is on the wire" and "the first check left" is the direct
       measure of the 0037 race fix: ~3.3 s (100 frames) before it, a few
       tens of milliseconds after it.

   The ICE watchdog is fixed as well: it used to live INSIDE the
   "candidate_pairs_num == 0" branch, and the reason function starts with
   `candidate_pairs_num == 0 -> "no_candidate_pairs"`, so the timeout line
   always said reason=no_candidate_pairs and the other three reasons
   could never be printed.  The watchdog now covers every shape of the
   failure, and the "all pairs failed" verdict keeps its own line.

4) REMB -- the bandwidth feedback a RECEIVER sends (R2: an AIMD estimate).

   We are the receiver.  A sender that gets no bandwidth feedback keeps
   its encoder at the starvation floor, which is the "smeared trickle"
   picture.  The RTCP PSFB (FMT=15) REMB is sent once per second, and
   the SDP negotiates "a=rtcp-fb:<pt> goog-remb" so that the peer
   expects it.  REMB is a PICTURE QUALITY feature, NOT a connectivity
   feature: a session without REMB still shows video, just at the
   starvation floor.

   History of the declared value (rembBitrateBps on the device):
     v0 sent the MEASURED rate -- a death spiral that could only go
        down (the device showed ~13800 against the official 3.6 Mbit/s);
     v1 (B1) declared max(measured, 8 Mbit/s) -- right direction, but a
        constant: it outruns this build's receive ceiling (~6.5 Mbit/s
        at 21 fps before the drain fix), and it has no congestion
        feedback at all;
     v2 (R2) declares an AIMD estimate driven by the receive-side stress
        counters:
          quiet window   -> declared + 0.5 Mbit/s (ask for more);
          stressed window (gaps / late drops / forced skips / dropped
          access units / SRTP decrypt failures at >= 2 percent of the
          window's packets) -> declared x 7/10 (back off);
          start 2 Mbit/s, clamped into [1 Mbit/s, 20 Mbit/s].
        That is what separates "the cloud throttles us" (quiet + low
        measured -> ask for MORE) from "we cannot keep up" (stress ->
        back OFF): the two states need OPPOSITE declarations, and the
        stress counters are the observable that tells them apart.
     v3 (R3, 2026-10-24 review) keeps the AIMD and refines it in three
        ways:
          the five stress counters split into NET stress (gaps / late
          drops / SRTP decrypt failures -- still the only driver of the
          multiplicative decrease) and SELF stress (forced skips /
          dropped access units -- diagnostic only, because backing the
          declaration off cannot stop us from dropping our own frames);
          the ceiling drops from 20 Mbit/s to 8 Mbit/s;
          the first 5 windows are additive-only (warmup), so the
          declaration climbs to a sane level before a decrease can fire.
        The review also asked to clamp the declaration with the measured
        rate (median over recent windows); that clamp is NOT adopted --
        it would pin the declaration at the floor exactly while the
        cloud starves us, which is the v0 death spiral again (see the
        comment next to PEER_REMB_CEIL_BPS for the full reasoning).

   New diagnostics (the remb_state prefix needs an engine whitelist
   key; see the delivery note):
     rtcp_remb sent=.. bitrateBps=<declared> measuredBps=<measured>
                  stressEv=<net+self> netStress=<net> selfStress=<self>
                  warmup=<0|1> windowPkts=<pkts> dir=start|ai|md
     remb_state declared=.. measuredLast=.. stressTotal=..
               keyframeAgoMs=..   (every 2 s; task D, observation only)
   Read remb_state against rtcp_remb: declared climbing while measured
   stays flat and stressTotal stays 0 means the cloud does not follow
   our REMB at all -- the "receiver estimation is not the bottleneck"
   verdict, readable directly on the device.

Note
----
This patch contains no key material of any kind.
